course: dictionary
lesson: 4185
title: A gentle word for God
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words show surprise without saying anything rude. «Golly» is one of them. Long ago, people thought it was wrong to say God's name in anger, so they bent the sound of it. God became «golly» and «gosh».
explain: Use the context to find the meaning. Someone who says «golly!» is surprised, delighted or shocked, not cross. It is an exclamation, a word that shows a strong feeling, and it often comes at the start of a sentence with an exclamation mark.
explain: Golly can also be used for fun, to sound innocent or silly, as in «good golly». Grandparents and characters in old children's books and cartoons often use it. To check a meaning, swap in a word like «wow». If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right idea.
fact: Golly is an exclamation, a word used to show a strong feeling such as surprise.
fact: «Golly» is an old-fashioned, mild word used instead of «God».
fact: Long ago, people bent words like God's name so they did not say it in anger. God became «golly» and «gosh».
fact: Other bent words: Jesus became «gee» and «jeez», and Christ became «crikey» and «cripes».
fact: Used for fun, golly sounds innocent, as in «good golly».
exercise: «Golly, look at that huge rainbow!» What feeling does golly show? | Sleepiness | *Surprise and delight | Anger at someone | Hunger
exercise: Golly is an old-fashioned word used instead of which word? | Goal | Gorilla | Gold | *God
exercise: Which word was bent from Jesus's name? | Crikey | Golly | *Gee | Gosh
test: Why did people change God's name into «golly» long ago? | *So they did not say God's name in anger | So it sounded like a bird | So they could sing it in church | Because it was a new language
test: What does «good golly» sound like? | A rude word for a bad temper | A question asking for help | *Silly and innocent, said for fun | The name of a mountain
exam: Which sentence uses golly as an exclamation? | The golly is in the box. | The dog ran home. | Golly is a kind of cat. | *Golly, that cake looks huge!
